# Settings for the personal install. Copy to .env (git ignores it) and fill in every value marked
# REQUIRED. This file holds secrets once filled in: keep it private, back it up with your passwords.
# The scripts here read .env; so does docker compose. Use values without spaces or quotes unless
# noted, and generate each secret with the command given.

# --- The database ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED. The database owner's password (runs migrations and backups). Only the postgres and
# migrate containers see it. Generate: openssl rand -hex 24
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
# REQUIRED. The password of the wambito_app role the API connects as. The migrate step creates the
# role with it and sets it again on every start, so it can be changed here later. 16+ characters.
# Letters and digits only (it goes into a URL). Generate: openssl rand -hex 24
APP_DB_PASSWORD=
# Name of the Docker volume that holds the database. Leave as is. Never delete this volume.
# DB_VOLUME_NAME=wambito-personal-db

# --- Sign-in -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# REQUIRED. Signs session cookies and codes. Generate: openssl rand -hex 32
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=
# Optional. Who may sign in: comma-separated emails, any letter case. Anyone else who asks for a
# code is refused and no email is sent. Passkeys of existing users keep working. Unset or empty:
# anyone may create an account.
# SIGN_IN_ALLOWLIST=you@example.com

# --- The Android app ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Lets the app open sign-in links and use passkeys for this hostname. The SHA-256 fingerprint of the
# certificate the app is signed with, as release.sh prints it (AB:CD:...:EF, 32 pairs). Several keys
# (yours, F-Droid's later): comma-separated. Empty: /.well-known/assetlinks.json is `[]` and the API
# accepts passkeys from the web only. Read when the web and api containers start: after a change,
# run ./up.sh. The API refuses to start on a value that is not a fingerprint.
# ANDROID_CERT_SHA256=
# The same for the iOS app: the 10-character Apple team id (letters and digits) it is signed with.
# Lets the app open sign-in links and use passkeys for this hostname. Empty: no iOS links or
# passkeys (/.well-known/apple-app-site-association has empty lists). Read when the web container
# starts: after a change, run ./up.sh. A value that is not a team id is skipped, with a warning.
# IOS_TEAM_ID=
# Guest sign-up from the app: an account without an email, whatever SIGN_IN_ALLOWLIST says. On by
# default; false stops new guests (existing ones keep working). Guests idle for 60 days are deleted
# once a day, with their data; accounts with an email never are.
# GUEST_SIGNUP=true

# --- Where it lives ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The public hostname. The app, the API (/api) and the passkeys are tied to it: passkeys made for
# one hostname do not work on another (email codes do). Through the tunnel, it must be the name you
# routed with `cloudflared tunnel route dns`; in the direct way, Caddy gets its certificate for it.
PUBLIC_HOSTNAME=app.wambito.com
# How the internet reaches this install. Unset: through a Cloudflare tunnel (TUNNEL_ID below), for a
# machine with no public address of its own, such as a Mac at home. On a server that has one, set
#   COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.direct.yml
# and it is served directly: Caddy answers on ports 80 and 443 itself, with a Let's Encrypt
# certificate for PUBLIC_HOSTNAME, redirects HTTP to HTTPS and sends HSTS; the client's address is
# the TCP peer's, whatever headers it sends; cloudflared is not started and TUNNEL_ID is not needed.
# The scripts here read it, and so does `docker compose` typed in this folder; it needs Docker
# Compose 2.24.4 or newer (`docker compose version`), for the override's `!override`. DNS: an A
# record for PUBLIC_HOSTNAME to the server, DNS only (not proxied), and no AAAA yet (README).
# Firewall: Docker publishes these ports around ufw, so filter outside the machine (Hetzner's Cloud
# Firewall: 22/tcp from your address, 80/tcp, 443/tcp, 443/udp). README, "Moving to a server".
# COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.yml:docker-compose.direct.yml
# The id of the tunnel (a UUID that `cloudflared tunnel create wambito-personal` prints). Its
# credentials file must be ~/.cloudflared/<TUNNEL_ID>.json. Not needed for `up.sh --no-tunnel`, nor
# in the direct way.
TUNNEL_ID=
# Where cloudflared keeps that file. Default: ~/.cloudflared
# CLOUDFLARED_DIR=

# --- Email (Resend on a server, Proton Bridge on a Mac) ------------------------------------------
# Sign-in codes go out through one of two. On a server, Resend is the way: set RESEND_API_KEY and
# leave SMTP_URL empty (exactly one of the two). On a Mac, Proton Bridge listens on 127.0.0.1:1025
# of the host and is reached from the containers as host.docker.internal:1025.
# REQUIRED on a Mac:
# smtp://USER:PASSWORD@host.docker.internal:1025
#   USER     the address you send from, with @ written as %40: you%40example.com
#   PASSWORD the "Password" Bridge shows for that account (Bridge > account > Mailbox configuration).
#            It is not your Proton password. Write any of  @ : / ? # %  as %40 %3A %2F %3F %23 %25.
# Example: smtp://you%40example.com:BRIDGE_PASSWORD@host.docker.internal:1025
SMTP_URL=
# RESEND_API_KEY=
# Bridge's certificate is self-signed. true accepts it (STARTTLS is still required). The API only
# allows this for a mail server on this machine (localhost, 127.0.0.1, host.docker.internal), so
# leave it false (or unset) with Resend.
SMTP_TLS_INSECURE=true
MAIL_FROM=Wambito <hola@wambito.com>

# --- Updates -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# up.sh and update.sh build what is checked out in this repository and apply its migrations to your
# real data, so they refuse to run on any other branch than this one, or with uncommitted changes
# to tracked files, unless you pass --force. Becomes main when the install follows main.
# PERSONAL_BRANCH=main
# The automatic deploy on a server (auto-deploy.sh, README "Automatic deploy"): the CI checks that
# must pass before a commit of PERSONAL_BRANCH is deployed, and the GitHub repository to ask
# (default: from the origin URL).
# DEPLOY_REQUIRED_CHECKS=check e2e
# DEPLOY_GITHUB_REPO=slinqer/wambito

# --- This machine ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where Caddy listens on plain HTTP for checking the install from this machine (smoke.sh), in
# either way: http://127.0.0.1:8088. Only this machine can reach it.
# WEB_PORT=8088
# The first three numbers of the private network this stack uses (x.x.x.0/24). The API trusts the
# web container at x.x.x.10. Change it only if it clashes with another network on your machine.
# NETWORK_PREFIX=172.28.77
# Where backup.sh puts its dumps. Keep it outside the repository, in a folder of its own.
# BACKUP_DIR=~/Backups/wambito
# Optional encrypted second copy of every backup (needs `age`: brew install age). Set BOTH:
#   BACKUP_COPY_DIR        a folder outside this Mac's only disk, e.g. iCloud Drive:
#                          ~/Library/Mobile Documents/com~apple~CloudDocs/Wambito
#   BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT   an age PUBLIC key (age1...) from `age-keygen -o wambito-backup.key`.
#                          Keep the file (the private key) in your password manager, not here.
# BACKUP_COPY_DIR=
# BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENT=
# Optional: the encrypted copy also goes to a Cloudflare R2 bucket (needs `rclone`, and the two
# settings above). The bucket's lifecycle rule deletes objects after 14 days. See README,
# "Backups in Cloudflare R2". Set all four, or none:
#   BACKUP_R2_BUCKET               the bucket's name, e.g. wambito-backups
#   BACKUP_R2_ENDPOINT             https://<account id>.r2.cloudflarestorage.com
#   BACKUP_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID        from an R2 API token with Object Read & Write on that bucket only
#   BACKUP_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY
# BACKUP_R2_BUCKET=
# BACKUP_R2_ENDPOINT=
# BACKUP_R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# BACKUP_R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# The age private key, only where you restore from a copy (never on the server): restore.sh
# --identity=FILE does the same.
# BACKUP_AGE_IDENTITY=
# Where `/`, `/en` and the roadmap redirect: the public landing site.
# LANDING_URL=https://wambito.com

# Admin panel (see docs/admin.md). Empty or unset: the panel is off.
# Comma-separated user ids that may use it.
# ADMIN_USER_IDS=
# Optional comma-separated IPs/CIDRs it answers to; unset: any address.
# ADMIN_ALLOWED_IPS=
